#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084318} :activity/id #uuid "66462250-9bf6-5b79-837b-72d46f60efbf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050867 :topic/id #uuid "6cb8c050-5f50-5c96-a3dc-144c8d409165" :topic/math-academy-id 753} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084318 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084499 :tutorial/content "Consider the mapping diagram of the function $f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/41/415b9ce71454f2aec9a2fc678c79f1d34fbb16468fbac50fc62b76283b9c209a.png)\n\n \n\nLet's now reverse the arrows, as follows:\n\n![](images/80/801b3eca4820b110c2145bd13fbf1faddbb0db83905869410cad1dc8a32f4e83.png)\n\n \n\nNow, we have a mapping diagram that \"reverses\" the action of the function $f.$ The diagram itself represents a new function, which we call the **inverse of the function $f,$** and denote by $f^{-1}.$\n\nWe can evaluate the inverse function $f^{-1}$ at any point in the range of $f.$ For example, to find the value of $f^{-1}(2),$ we notice that we have an arrow from ${\\color{black}2}$ (on the right) to ${\\color{black}0}$ (on the left). Therefore,\n\n$$\nf^{-1}({\\color{black}2}) = {\\color{black}0}.\n$$\n\nIn the original function, $f,$ that same arrow went from ${\\color{black}0}$ (on the left) to ${\\color{black}2}$ (on the right). So, we have that $f({\\color{black}0})={\\color{black}2},$  and as a result, we obtain\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(f(0)) = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, applying the function $f$ and then \"reversing\" the action using $f^{-1}$ will get us back to $0.$\n\nIn general, this is true for every $x$ in the domain of the function $f\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(f(x)) = x\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "05fd6236-8b7b-59d7-bf60-5e7d9d2db65a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6883 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f836d465-3cfd-57b6-ab05-79619fabc9a6" :step/math-academy-id 41715 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084319}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084319 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083356 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082904 :question/id #uuid "bca42c78-0735-587e-914b-7c9892f47d51" :question/math-academy-id "e-10986"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2b6ea7-ea1c-5d6b-b786-bb0880c6af4a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050633}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521506}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Inverse Function From a Mapping Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "2e3f4334-5c00-58e4-9032-fed944bab672" :step/math-academy-id 41716 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084320}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084320 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083357 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082905 :question/id #uuid "841640c2-1f50-5a13-b12b-bfe202e7621c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10987"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd1735b2-8684-5312-8315-ec6acb2daa21" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521521}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Inverse Function Given Function Data"} :step/id #uuid "d19f2942-df0f-5896-adf0-8c5b3ca192b5" :step/math-academy-id 41719 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084321}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084321 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083358 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082906 :question/id #uuid "200ba5c0-9ec6-56e9-8ce4-24cee169ac8c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10988"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab89a7ad-8b73-5dde-b5ed-efac2deb67e1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048838}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521568}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Inverse Function Using a Graph"} :step/id #uuid "26073589-e276-519e-b495-acab562e4cb3" :step/math-academy-id 41722 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084322}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084322 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084500 :tutorial/content "Recall that the ![](images/c5/c594a328984a211291c3547317baeb153ed97e2db7f43ab25ce204c31fe1796c.svg) \"reverses\" the action of ![](images/bc/bc562d54615dad55d095db2afed7626f62ff7ebf0ab6c5d45471b77ac30b6398.svg) so that\n![](images/ea/eab0458598a0b4298ccbfcd1664babf085309c0017ba0e530c76900f38bb8678.svg)\nwhich can be visualized using the mapping diagram, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3d/3dd33f3f90eaadec2f1168db89665d1986e562005fc01cd9f58dbc3f17e7a616.png)\n\n \n\nSince ![](images/aa/aabc5ff96d4807ff058c8f3e9f78aa70ced5e624ac2ae1ebdd1bb4597d6c6dad.svg) is just the composition ![](images/5a/5aeac36928f2e7a97b7c71c2c97dd9bef3774970b3efc871c9131c6d2075be5e.svg) we can now give the algebraic definition of the inverse. \n\nThe function ![](images/c5/c594a328984a211291c3547317baeb153ed97e2db7f43ab25ce204c31fe1796c.svg) is called the **inverse of the function ![](images/bc/bc562d54615dad55d095db2afed7626f62ff7ebf0ab6c5d45471b77ac30b6398.svg)** if\n![](images/52/52a4e32d6165988fe50c0766a1310fd3afb895f4a11149ff7b45c1cb0dd76e3a.svg)\nfor each ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) in the domain of ![](images/a0/a047a10a3ed3f5e86676e8fca21093abfe6a1849d90ce94b62101f34fca37e3d.svg)\n\nIn other words, to show that two functions ![](images/93/93f888a1ee0b7da5e29f442f803f1c0eaf73ad02f5eaa97e85bb8a35d607015b.svg) and ![](images/a9/a9e111e418e432fd154a819bb521c373ca64e9b27bbeed17a5777bba6940c6b6.svg) are inverses of each other, we need to check the following condition:\n![](images/7f/7facf5a55f1e90f9b9aac2358e4bc4b0560c2bd4b9fa2700e902a4dde84f90b7.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "f4a2e52b-5aee-58ed-bcc3-b236246d862c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6884 :tutorial/title "The Definition of the Inverse Function"} :step/id #uuid "3130ad01-93cf-56c2-a59d-52d2324277cf" :step/math-academy-id 41725 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084323}} {:db/id #edb/ref 1